::CW_U06_L05_GP_Q01::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A student is preparing a poem for digital publication. Which formatting choice best supports the genre?{
~A. Put the entire poem in one paragraph so it uses less space.#Incorrect. A poem usually needs intentional line breaks and stanza breaks so the reader can follow image, sound, and pacing.
~B. Add random symbols before every line.#Incorrect. Decoration can distract readers if it does not support meaning.
~C. Remove the title because poems do not need one.#Incorrect. A title often prepares the reader for the poem's focus or tone.
=D. Keep the title clear and preserve purposeful line and stanza breaks.#Correct. This matches the poem's genre and helps the reader see how the poem moves.
}

::CW_U06_L05_GP_Q02::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A short story draft appears as one long block of text with dialogue inside the same paragraph. What should the writer do first?{
~A. Change every sentence to a different font.#Incorrect. Font changes do not solve the readability problem.
~B. Remove all dialogue marks.#Incorrect. This would make the dialogue harder to follow.
=C. Add paragraph breaks when the speaker changes and when the action shifts.#Correct. Paragraphing helps readers follow dialogue, action, and pacing in prose.
~D. Add extra blank lines after every sentence.#Incorrect. Random spacing can make the piece look unfinished.
}

::CW_U06_L05_GP_Q03::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which item best belongs in a final digital readiness check?{
=A. Whether the file has a clear title, consistent spacing, and no leftover draft notes.#Correct. These checks help the piece look finished and readable.
~B. Whether the writer can explain every idea orally after submission.#Incorrect. The final digital version should be understandable without extra oral explanation.
~C. Whether the writer used the brightest possible color.#Incorrect. Color is not a substitute for clarity and format readiness.
~D. Whether the piece is longer than everyone else's.#Incorrect. Length does not prove publication readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L05_GP_Q04::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A script has character names written in several different styles. Which revision best improves publication formatting?{
~A. Delete all character names.#Incorrect. Character names help the reader know who speaks.
=B. Use one consistent style for character labels and separate stage directions from dialogue.#Correct. Consistency helps readers or performers follow the scene.
~C. Add more stage directions without checking labels.#Incorrect. More content does not fix inconsistent formatting.
~D. Change the script into a poem.#Incorrect. The task is to format the script, not change genres.
}

::CW_U06_L05_GP_Q05::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which publication note best explains reader effect?{
~A. I changed the format because it looked cool.#Incorrect. This does not explain how the format supports the reader.
~B. I used many decorations because publication means fancy.#Incorrect. Publication quality means reader-ready, not randomly decorated.
~C. I did not check spacing because the story is already finished.#Incorrect. Final formatting is part of preparing a finished piece.
=D. I revised paragraph breaks and dialogue spacing so the reader can follow the scene without rereading.#Correct. This names specific formatting choices and explains the reader effect.
}
